[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that's the model that doesn't need replacing.

  Next big fork: [1mdelivery[0m. This one drives infrastructure more than any other choice, because it decides whether the
  system can be purely synchronous or needs a background worker.

  - [1mIn-app only[0m — a bell icon and a feed. Notifications are just rows you read on page load. No queue, no worker, no
    external provider, no deliverability problems. But nothing reaches anyone who isn't looking at the tab.
  - [1mIn-app + email[0m — adds reach, and adds real machinery: a job queue, retries, an email provider, bounce handling,
    unsubscribe links, and the whole question of how not to send 40 emails an hour. This is where most of the
    engineering cost in notification systems actually goes.
  - [1mIn-app + browser push[0m — reaches people who closed the tab, without an email provider. Costs a service worker, a
    permission prompt users often decline, and per-browser subscription storage.

  Cheap to add later: email, [3mif[0m the event and recipient model is built cleanly now — the notification row is the same
  row either way, only the delivery step differs. Expensive to retrofit: nothing here, really, which is why I'd start
  narrow.

